+  "rule": "protocol/environments.md section 8.4.1: a failed read, stat, or parse of any state or surface file is never reported as absence. Each case pins one file class (marker, lock, provisioning seed, credential passthrough entry) to its typed, present-or-absent discriminating inputs and the expected diagnostic. Failure classes: permission-denied (EACCES-class), io-error (EIO-class), unparseable-content (bytes fail decoding or parsing), schema-invalid-content (parsed content fails schema validation for a reason other than version), symlink-where-regular-required, directory-where-file-expected, parent-not-directory (ENOTDIR-class: the entry exists but a path component above it is not a directory, so the open fails). presence/entry_kind are ground truth: permission-denied and io-error strike at the open/read stage after lstat. Boundary verification precedes readability classification (sections 1.3, 4, 8.2, 10.1): a marker failure that violates the section 4 contract, or that prevents proving the boundary (symlink, directory, or unstatable marker path), is environment_store_untrusted and is outside this family; the marker cases therefore cover the open/read/parse stages only. Seed unparseable-content applies where the seed path parses (the codex_cli revision-B mcp_servers strip, which MUST parse as TOML); elsewhere seeds are unparsed byte copies. Passthrough entries carry no content and require a link, so unparseable-content, schema-invalid-content, and symlink-where-regular-required do not apply to them; a directory at the entry path is 'no longer a symlink' and stays detached-side. currency 'unknown' pins the section 8.4.1 rule that an unreadable file makes the affected row non-current with currency unknown; absence-side outcomes carry currency 'known'. repair_relinks pins that --repair re-links a detached entry and leaves an unreadable entry untouched.",
